  <p style="color: #8888A0; margin: 0; font-size: 14px;">The new rule that lets the tax agency act without going to court first - and why preparation is cheap</p>
</div>

<div style="width: 60px; height: 3px; background: linear-gradient(90deg, #4ECDC4, transparent); margin: 48px 0 40px; border-radius: 2px;"></div>

<h2>What actually changed</h2>

<p>President Tinubu signed four tax reform bills into law in June 2025. They took effect January 1, 2026. One of those four - the Nigeria Tax Administration Act (NTAA) - contains a specific provision at Section 60 that is much more significant than its placement suggests.</p>

<p>In plain English: <strong>the Nigeria Revenue Service (the renamed FIRS) can now freeze any business bank account without first obtaining a court order</strong>, once the business has what the law calls an "established" tax debt.</p>

<p>Before 2026, a freeze required court involvement. A judge looked at the case. You had time to respond. Both sides argued. The process took weeks or months.</p>

<p>From January 2026 forward, if the NRS determines you have an established tax debt - which includes unfiled returns, late filings, or underreported income - they can send a directive straight to your bank. Your account is frozen until the debt is cleared.</p>

<blockquote style="border-left: 4px solid #4ECDC4; padding: 16px 24px; margin: 32px 0; background: rgba(78, 205, 196, 0.05); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;">
  <p style="font-size: 20px; font-style: italic; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">The court wasn't removed to punish you. It was removed to make enforcement faster. The practical effect for your business is the same.</p>
</blockquote>

<div style="width: 60px; height: 3px; background: linear-gradient(90deg, #4ECDC4, transparent); margin: 48px 0 40px; border-radius: 2px;"></div>

<h2>Why this was put in place (and why it will be used)</h2>

<p>The 2026 Nigerian federal budget projects total expenditure of <strong>₦58.18 trillion</strong> and revenue collection of <strong>₦34.33 trillion</strong>. As of Q3 2025, Nigeria's actual revenue tracked at about ₦18.6 trillion. Do the math. The Federal Government needs to materially close a gap of roughly <strong>30 to 49 percent year-over-year</strong> between 2025 performance and 2026 targets.</p>

<p>That is the budget math. It does not need a conspiracy theory. The Nigeria Revenue Service was redesigned specifically to close this gap, and Section 60 is one of the tools they were given to do it.</p>

<p>They will use it. Not maliciously, and not against businesses that are clearly compliant. They will use it against businesses with accumulated debts, missing filings, or mismatched declarations. The problem is <strong>most Nigerian SMEs have at least one of those three today and do not realize it</strong>.</p>

<div style="width: 60px; height: 3px; background: linear-gradient(90deg, #4ECDC4, transparent); margin: 48px 0 40px; border-radius: 2px;"></div>

<h2>The AI layer most owners have not considered</h2>

<p>Before 2026, underreporting income worked because Nigerian tax enforcement was manual. An officer had to compare your filings to your bank activity and notice a discrepancy. That took time, and frankly, they did not do it much.</p>

<p>Starting this year, the Nigeria Revenue Service runs AI-driven cross-referencing. The system compares:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Your declared business income</li>
  <li>Actual bank inflows across your linked accounts</li>
  <li>Payroll filings from your own business</li>
  <li>Invoices your customers filed as expenses on their end</li>
  <li>Any payment-processor data that reaches regulatory reporting</li>
</ul>

<p>When the AI finds a discrepancy it considers material, the case goes to a queue for enforcement. Section 60 is the button they press when the debt is established and the taxpayer has not responded to requests to settle.</p>

<p>This is not dystopian. It is standard in most OECD countries already. What is notable is that Nigeria implemented it in one step - courts to AI, no middle stage.</p>

<div style="width: 60px; height: 3px; background: linear-gradient(90deg, #4ECDC4, transparent); margin: 48px 0 40px; border-radius: 2px;"></div>

<h2>Who is affected (almost certainly you)</h2>

<p>If any of the following are true about your Nigerian business, Section 60 applies to you:</p>

<ul>
  <li>You have a business bank account that receives customer payments</li>
  <li>You receive money from customers via transfers, POS, or payment processors</li>
  <li>You have registered with CAC (Corporate Affairs Commission)</li>
  <li>You or your business has a TIN (Tax Identification Number)</li>
  <li>Your business generated more than ₦100 million in turnover last year</li>
</ul>

<p>Small companies under ₦100 million turnover are exempt from Companies Income Tax under the new regime, which is a real relief. <strong>But they are not exempt from filing returns, maintaining TIN records, or the vendor verification rules.</strong> The bank-freezing power can still be triggered by unfiled returns even if no income tax is owed. That is the subtlety that catches operators.</p>

<blockquote style="border-left: 4px solid #4ECDC4; padding: 16px 24px; margin: 32px 0; background: rgba(78, 205, 196, 0.05); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;">
  <p style="font-size: 20px; font-style: italic; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">Being exempt from paying tax is not the same as being exempt from reporting. Section 60 triggers on both.</p>
</blockquote>

<div style="width: 60px; height: 3px; background: linear-gradient(90deg, #4ECDC4, transparent); margin: 48px 0 40px; border-radius: 2px;"></div>

<h2>The 5 things smart operators are doing this month</h2>

<p>None of these are expensive. None require a tax lawyer. All of them can be done in about 3 hours of total effort spread across a week.</p>

<h3>1. Verify your TIN is active and correct</h3>

<p>Go to the Nigeria Revenue Service TIN verification portal. Enter your business TIN. Confirm the status returns "Active." If it shows "Dormant," "Unregistered," or returns an error, <strong>resolve that this week</strong>, not next quarter. An inactive TIN is the most common reason businesses get caught in enforcement nets they did not know existed.</p>

<p>The fix for an inactive TIN is usually just filing your most recent overdue return. The hardest part is noticing the problem.</p>

<h3>2. File any outstanding returns, even if your tax liability is zero</h3>

<p>Small companies under the ₦100 million threshold do not owe Companies Income Tax, but they still need to file the return that confirms they were under the threshold. A missing return, not missing money, is what triggers the "unfiled" classification. Many zero-tax businesses are walking around thinking they are in the clear when they are actually non-filers in NRS's database.</p>

<p>Filing is free. The penalty for not filing is ₦100,000 in the first month and ₦50,000 every month afterward. It compounds. Two years of not filing can reach several hundred thousand naira in penalties on a business that owed zero tax.</p>

<h3>3. Check your recent bank activity matches your declared income</h3>

<p>Pull your last 12 months of bank statements. Add up inflows. Compare to what you told NRS in your last return. If the bank number is significantly higher, you have one of two things happening: either family/personal money is flowing through your business account (fix this now by separating accounts), or you under-reported income. Either way, deal with it before the AI finds it.</p>

<p>An unprompted amendment filed by you looks very different to the NRS from an error they discovered. The first results in back-tax owed. The second results in back-tax plus penalty plus potential fraud investigation.</p>

<h3>4. Verify every vendor you pay has a valid TIN</h3>

<p>This is the rule almost no one is talking about yet. Under the new law, <strong>if you pay a vendor or service provider who does not have a valid TIN, you (the hiring business) face a ₦5 million penalty per vendor</strong>, not the vendor.</p>

<p>Imagine you paid a freelance designer, a consultant, or a small printing vendor last year. If any of them does not have a TIN, the NRS can fine you ₦5 million per unregistered vendor when they audit your bank transfers.</p>

<p>The fix is simple but tedious: pull your list of everyone you paid in the last 12 months. Ask each one for their TIN. Verify each against the NRS lookup. Any that fail, either help them register or stop working with them.</p>

<p>This is the single highest-value 2-hour task a Nigerian business owner can do this quarter. We are considering building a tool at Pejji's sister company Securva that does this automatically against a vendor list you upload. If that would save you time, let us know - we will prioritize it.</p>

<h3>5. Separate business and personal money properly</h3>

<p>If your business account is the same one you use for Uber rides and school fees, NRS's AI will not know which is which. It will default to "assume the larger number is the income." Open a dedicated business account if you have not. Route business revenue through it. Pay yourself a defined salary from that account into a separate personal one.</p>

<p>This one habit alone probably saves more Nigerian businesses from accidental enforcement than any other single change.</p>

<div style="width: 60px; height: 3px; background: linear-gradient(90deg, #4ECDC4, transparent); margin: 48px 0 40px; border-radius: 2px;"></div>

<h2>What you gain by doing this in April rather than in October</h2>

<p>The first high-profile Section 60 bank account freeze will happen in 2026. It will make front-page news. Every Nigerian business owner will panic. Compliance services will get booked out. Accountants will raise their rates.</p>

<p>If you do the 5 things above in April, you are a spectator when that happens, not a participant. Your returns are filed. Your TIN is active. Your vendors are verified. Your bank activity matches your declarations.</p>

<p>More importantly, you are the operator who saw this coming. That position compounds in every way your business interacts with partners, investors, and larger customers going forward. <strong>People buy from and work with operators who look like they have things handled.</strong></p>

